/*--------------------------------*- Choupo -*-----------------------*\
  Utility: ammonia refrigeration, DIRECT EXPANSION at atmospheric pressure.

  Sources: derived here from records in this tree -- see COST below.  The
    duty is thermodynamic and exact; the PRICE is an ESTIMATE and says so.

  When to use:
    For process duties needing a cold side below about -25 C, where
    `refrigerationPG` stops applying.  That boundary is not this record's
    opinion: refrigerationPG.dat states it itself -- "below -25 C the
    secondary loop becomes unattractive -- switch to direct refrigerant" --
    and until this file existed the catalogue named that switch and offered
    nothing to switch TO.  A duty at -23 C was refused with "(none
    adequate)", correctly, and left out of C_UT entirely.

    In an ammonia plant this is the natural cold utility rather than an
    imported one: NH3 is bled from the synthesis loop, expanded, evaporated
    against the loop gas and recompressed.  The refrigerant is the product,
    so a leak from the cold side into the process is not a contamination.
    -33 C is also the atmospheric storage condition, so the plant already
    holds this level.

  WHY THE MARGIN IS TIGHT, and what breaks it:
    `pickForDuty` requires T_in <= T_process - dTmin.  At the 250 K duty
    this record was written for, the margin is 250 - 239.82 = 10.18 K
    against a default dTmin of 10 K.  RAISING dTmin above 10.18 K, or
    raising the process temperature, drops the allocation back to "(none
    adequate)" -- which is the honest answer, but a reader who has not read
    this paragraph will not know why it moved.

  MECHANISM, and a fact worth knowing before trusting the word:
    This is the FIRST record in the tree with a phase change on the COLD
    side (the three existing cooling utilities are all `sensible`).  The
    word is validated by `UtilityCatalogue` and then only PRINTED, into a
    column of reports/utilities/utilities.csv -- nothing in the allocation
    branches on it.  `dutyPerKg` carries the whole physics whatever the
    mechanism, so an evaporation record works exactly as a sensible one
    does.  Stated because the enum accepting the word is NOT evidence that
    the path is exercised, and this is the first record that exercises it.

  T_out EQUALS T_in, because a boiling refrigerant changes phase and does
    not rise in temperature.  Safe: `Utility::T_out` is parsed and read by
    nothing in the allocation (the `T_out` in UtilityAllocationReport is the
    PROCESS unit's KPI, a different quantity sharing a name).
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

utility     refrigerationNH3;
tier        cooling;
mechanism   evaporation;               // boiling refrigerant; no temperature rise

components  ( NH3 );
state       saturatedLiquid;
P           1.013 bar;                 // atmospheric; sets the level
T_in        239.82 K;                  // -33.33 C -- NH3 normal boiling point
T_out       239.82 K;                  // evaporation: same temperature

// Energy absorbed per kg = the latent heat at Tb, from the component's own
// record: NH3.dat HvapTb 23330 J/mol / MW 17.030 kg/kmol.
// The tree stores no derivative, but a utility record's dutyPerKg is the
// utility's OWN primary datum in the form every sibling uses (Cp x dT there,
// Hvap here), so it is written, with the arithmetic shown.
dutyPerKg   1369935.0;                 // J/kg  (23330 / 0.017030)

// COST -- AN ESTIMATE, DERIVED IN THE OPEN, NOT A CITED PRICE.
//   No published table for a -33 C level was available to the curator, and
//   inventing a citation would convert `unsourced` into `falsely sourced`,
//   which no reader and no gate can detect.  So it is derived from records
//   in this same directory, and every input is named:
//
//     COP_Carnot = T_c/(T_h - T_c) = 239.82/(308.15 - 239.82) = 3.5097
//       T_c = this record's T_in
//       T_h = coolingWater.dat T_out (308.15 K), the heat sink declared here
//     COP_real   = 3.5097 x 0.50 = 1.7549
//       0.50 = second-law efficiency.  A DECLARED ASSUMPTION, not a cited
//       one; it is the only number here with no record behind it.
//     electricity per GJ of cooling = 1/1.7549 = 0.5698 GJ
//     cost = 0.5698 x 27.8 = 15.84 EUR/GJ
//       27.8 = electricity.dat cost, EUR/GJ, costYear 2022
//
//   The value is a LOWER BOUND: a real condenser condenses above the
//   cooling-water RETURN, so the true T_h is higher, the true COP lower and
//   the true cost higher.
//
//   INTERNAL CROSS-CHECK (not a validation): the catalogue's cooling prices
//   rise monotonically as the level drops -- 0.4 (25 C) < 4.0 (7 C) <
//   6.0 (0 C) < 15.84 (-33 C) -- which is the Carnot penalty
//   refrigerationPG.dat's own note predicts.
//
//   THIS VALUE IS NOT CURATED.  Promotion to a cited price is the owner's
//   review, and a `reviewStatus` key is deliberately NOT written here: no
//   utility record carries one and `UtilityCatalogue` does not read it, so
//   it would be a field the engine cannot see -- a comment in the wrong
//   place.  The status is prose because prose is where this form puts it.
cost        15.84;                     // €/GJ removed -- DERIVED, see above
costYear    2022;                      // inherited from its electricity input
description "Ammonia direct-expansion refrigeration (-33 C, atmospheric)";
